r  help comes with our from our clinicians  that need guidance  comes from the community who's looking  to us  and not infrequently it's coming from  the government who imposes certain  regulations for us that we need to  follow regarding opioids and other  substances  now hopefully you do have an opiate  stewardship program in your hospital i  was actually just involved with another  talk and i learned that about 40 of  hospitals actually have an opioid  stewardship program so if you don't have  it yet get on it we actually put this  paper in the joint commission journal on  quality and patient safety in 2019 and  it's really a cookbook to describe how  you can pull together a team and make  this happen please do look that up we've  also got a website which is  bcore.brighamanwomens.org  and we've really worked hard to just  make as much as we can open access we  put a lot of guidelines and documents on  there feel free to use whatever you'd  like for your health system i find this  is this is something where the more we  share the more we learn from each other  that's really the spirit of this  there's kind of three main phases to  what needs to be done for opioid  stewardship in my opinion  the first is creation of system-wide  guidelines and for us we created two  separate documents one is for acute pain  and one's for chronic pain  and what we did is we took guidelines  such as the 2016 cdc guidelines for  treatment of chronic non-cancer pain we  took what was required for our state  laws and we put them together and  created documents and guidance that our  clinicians can use  we found that it was helpful to do this  because it really did set the  expectations both for the providers  and particularly for patients because  with patients that have have pain issues  if you're withholding opioids for what  for whatever reason because you want to  try and use non-opioid pain relief which  we know is evidence-based a lot of times  patients just don't want to feel like  they're being treated differently and so  part of the guidelines are to  standardize our treatment standardize  the care that we offer to our patients  it's also protective for the hospital  i'm hearing more and more cases where  hospitals are named in suits from  patients that had adverse outcomes after  opioids this shows that the hospital is  dedicated to to using those medications  responsibly  once you have guidelines the next step  is to make metrics and reports and  things that are actionable  for us we created a whole opioid  dashboard it's got ambulatory care  emergency department care and surgical  care  guidelines in there for ambulatory care  we were able to look at the numbers of  opiate prescriptions per 1000 patient  visits that was helpful for us to  address outlier practices within our  system  in the ed we look at things like the  percentage of patients who are leaving  with opioid prescriptions the numbers of  pills per prescription  and we've been able not necessarily to  reduce these numbers but to really  tighten the means so we're not we don't  have such uh such outliers just the  practice is a lot more consistent now  and finally surgical i'm going to talk  about at length a little bit later in  this talk  once you have metrics and reports then  you can create clinical decision tools  these are just a couple of examples that  we have one of them warns you if you've  reached more than 90 more few milligram  equivalents per day  another one reminds you if you don't  have an opiate treatment agreement on  file for patients that are on chronic  opioids  we have other ones that remind about  naloxone co-prescribing for patients on  high dose or chronic opioids we have one  that i  really like which is when a patient is  discharged after a cesarean section or  vaginal delivery if they receive an  opioid when they come back to clinic it  reminds the provider to ask them if  they've finished using that medication  and to discard it if it's no longer  needed which it frequently isn't after  delivery  and then finally we have one in the  upper right corner which is puts all of  it together it checks to make sure that  the provider has looked at the  prescription drug monitoring program  takes to make sure that there's an  opiate agreement on file make sure that  there's not opioids and benzodiazepines  together and really it's just a holistic  view that's used whenever a patient  provider is refilling a prescription for  opioids  so those are just the basics and now  again i want to dive into three specific  advanced concepts  the first one is post-surgical opiate  prescriptions  this is probably the most common  post-operative complication and i know  it's a little bit unusual to hear that  because we think of things like  infection or readmission is the most  common  but if you look at this about seven  percent of patients who work on opioids  before their surgery will be on  long-term opioids after their surgery  that's life alternate alternating that  means that they're going to be on these  medications for a long time and so even  though we want to we want to well at the  same time we want to treat pain  compassionately and often that involves  the use of opioids we also don't want to  be stuck in a situation where the  patient is then dependent on them and  uses it far beyond when their pain is  expected to subside  in fact there was this meta-analysis  from 33 studies that did find that seven  percent of patients continued to fill  opioids more than three months after  surgery  the second one was actually pretty  pretty stunning  analysis of six studies found that  between 67 to 92 percent of  post-surgical patients  had opioids that were unused after their  surgery  so they took some of the pills but they  didn't finish them  and then we found that 42 to 71 percent  were entirely unused the patient didn't  even use the prescription at all  yet about 15 to 29 depending on the  study reported opioid-induced adverse  effects  this just makes the case that we need to  right-size how we use opioids after  surgery  at our hospital we've released  guidelines  this is the guidelines for  after general surgery i think you'll  find it's pretty interesting  after you get your appendix out the most  we give is 10 pills same with  gallbladder  for the bigger surgeries like colectomy  we give up to 20 pills  this is a big change from what we were  doing years ago where we were getting 30  60 or 90 pills after discharge but what  we found is that patients do really well  with this they often don't need more  than that and what happens is that after  the acute pain subsides they're fine  using non-opioid pain medications they  don't have them sitting around in the  medicine cabinet while the where they  can be diverted or they don't use them  excessively to the point where they  develop tolerance and then withdrawals  they stop using them  if you're going to embark on this  journey i want to share two different  resources with you the first is a great  program out of michigan called open  and you can just google michigan open  project  this is a very rigorous evidence-based  approach in which they looked at all  sorts of surgical procedures  they looked at the prescriptions that  patients got they interviewed the  patients before and after their  surgeries and their use of opioids and  they found that  patients often didn't need the large  numbers that were being prescribed  this group publishes their guidelines  their recommendations and they update it  every year as well so this is a really  good place for you to start  evidence-based  and then the final part to this is about  education  patients need to be informed that  they're going to have pain after surgery  our job is to make it bearable and  manageable and that it will improve  but not that it's going to take it away  completely it's it's realistic to think  that you will have  again your gallbladder  open colectomy and that you're going to  have zero pain so managing and tempering  expectations is important in a way that  patients can  be comfortable even with non-opiate  medications but they might not have zero  pain until they heal  we've created our own post-surgical  guidelines there's also a very good set  of guidelines at the american college of  surgeons and you can see that on the on  the page here and you can freely  download this it's a fantastic resource  and you can just plug this into your  instructions tomorrow and get started  shifting gears we're going to talk about  tapering of high dose opiate  prescriptions  this has been a controversial topic and  it needs to be done very thoughtfully  you've all heard about  the the challenges of opioids and what  the cause of the opioid epidemic the  quote unquote epidemic has been  and we know that early on in the  epidemic  it was often attributed to prescribed  pain medications the pain is the fifth  vital sign etc we've increased our  prescriptions for opioids which i'll  show you on the next slide  but i thought this chart really captures  this nicely  the yellow are deaths attributed to  prescribed opioids  the orange are deaths attributed to  heroin  and the red is death attributed to  synthetic opioids like fentanyl  and the really interesting thing here is  that you can see that  the you know although we put more  attention for for several years on  heroin and then particularly these past  four to five years on synthetic opioids  as being the lion's share of reasons for  for having overdose you can see that  that yellow the amount of yellow really  hasn't budged  so although we are seeing increased  deaths from heroin and fentanyl the  numbers of deaths from prescribed  opioids is staying the same and it  essentially means that we can't lose  sight and can't lose track of the  prescribed opioids  i thought this was a fascinating chart  this looks up through through 2020  through last year  this is the prescribed or actually the  dispensed  opioids in the united states from 1992  to 2020.  and it basically converted all the  opioids to morphine milligram  equivalents you can there's conversion  factors where you can convert any opioid  to an equivalent of morphine  you can see back in 1992 we prescribed  about 25 billion morphine milligram  equivalents in this country which is  quite a lot actually  but in 2011 we were at 10 times that  rate 250 billion morphine milligram  equivalents  since that we've had increased  recognition of the over prescribing of  these medications and there's been a  marked decrease  and you can see that we've we have the  pendulum hazard actually that has swung  back tremendously we're now to the level  that we were at around 2001 or so  so um it's going to be interesting to  see where we settle out from this um and  my hope is that we don't overshoot it  because i think it was clear that we  were under treating pain in the 90s and  then we over treated it in the early  2000s we'll see where we settle  but if you actually look at the  prescriptions  for different doses you can see  something interesting the first is that  the total and this is from 2011 to 2019  decreased by about 50 percent  but the majority the the larger number  largest number of prescriptions that  decreased were for those of greater than  90 more few milligram equivalents today  per day they decreased by about 70  and probably the reason for this are the  cdc guidelines  in particular the cdc guidelines in 2016  came out said for non-cancer chronic  pain  you should avoid prescriptions over 90  mm per day unless there is careful  consideration and justification  at our hospital what that meant was that  we actually had a mandate from our  hospital board of directors to avoid a  high-dose prescriptions  when this happened they came to me as  the director of the opiate stewardship  program and said fix this  meanwhile i know that we have legacy  patients we have patients that were on  high-dose opioids well before the 2016  guidelines so we had to figure out what  to do  first thing was we looked backwards we  looked at from 2018 to 19 we had about  77 000 pill form op prescriptions  written  and about 15.7 percent were for more  than 90 more female grammatical ones  today per day and just that was very  eye-opening for us we didn't realize  that we had so many that were for high  dose and i would encourage you all to do  the same at your systems  but then when we drilled down deeper  we found that just two clinics were  responsible for nearly half of all of  that prescribing  and so we instantly knew where our  target was and again this is an example  of looking at the data and how that can  help you  so what we did is for those two clinics  we rolled out a comprehensive program  first of all starting in july 2019 our  trainees and fellows were prohibited for  writing opiate prescriptions for more  than 90 more for milligram of course per  day this basically did two things the  first is it did make it slightly more  inconvenient for the attending doctors  who then had how to write the  prescription and how to think about it  but it also set the stage for our  trainees that is no longer okay to write  these prescriptions  and i know this is controversial because  there are a lot of patients that have  chronic non-cancer pain that are on  these medications  and they they  need these medications to survive and to  to function  but we have found that if you do this in  a compassionate way the majority of  those patients can be tapered to safer  levels  and hopefully it should go without  saying that we know that the risk of  overdose death being on more than 90 to  100 more milligram coins a day is about  10 times higher than those who are on  much lower doses under 40 more milligram  equivalents today per day so it is it is  a safety issue for the patient it is a  best practice  so in addition to preventing  prescriptions for opioids at high doses  for our trainees we also promulgated  system-wide best practices as part of  those guidelines i mentioned before a  prescription drug monitoring program  check prior to every prescription  naloxone co-prescriptions for those on  chronic opioids pain treatment agreement  for everyone on chronic opioids  random toxicology screening at least  once a year  clinic visit every every three months  we rolled out safe tapering  recommendations  we avoided opioids of benzos and made  sure that they were not prescribed in  combination  and then we performed random chart  reviews every month to make sure that  the providers were following these  guidelines  we then shared the prescribing practices  with their peers so we could normalize  and tell people where where they're at  similar to what i mentioned from the  from the previous metric tools  we have a dedicated pharmacist who is  there to help safely tape her patients  she's wonderful she's passionate about  this and she actually has prescriptive  authority so she can write prescriptions  and really takes out  the the middleman essentially because  she can say we're going to be very  evidence-based very very algorithmic  about your paper and again not make  patients feel like they're being singled  out  we offered psychosocial support for  patients with difficulty with the papers  rapid access to psychiatry  rapid access to our substance use  disorder bridge clinic which i'll talk  about at the end of this talk and we had  monthly meetings with our chief medical  officer compliance and other leaders  and we're really pleased with the  results on this chart you can see the  blue the blue columns are prescriptions  for less than 90 morphe milligram  equivalents per day and the red columns  are those for 90 more milligram  equivalents per day and from when we  started doing the paper you can see a  marked decrease in the number of  of the the high dose opioids and not  much of a change of the lower dose  opioids which we're happy with we're  fine with  one thing to note though is that the the  after the intervention this is about 18  months of data  this is not something that's going to  happen overnight this is actually  something that takes place over months  frequently you're going to taper  around 10 percent of the dose per month  and that can be a long paper if a  patient is already on very high doses of  opioids  so for us we did find that this  multi-faceted multidisciplinary model  could safely reduce the numbers of  high-dose prescriptions and it can take  months it's also not appropriate for all  patients but we we try and justify when  it's not appropriate and we try and make  that a small number of patients  we want to find out what happened to  some of the patients that dropped out  that no longer were followed at the  clinic we don't know if there were  unintentional overdoses but our  preliminary analysis shows that there  were not  we  the provider experience too because i  think this was a big change for a lot of  the providers  we also started to look at the patient  experience and we actually look back at  our patient  uh the surveys that are sent out to  patients after their visits and  unfortunately the vast majority of them  are satisfied  because we found that with this approach  patients do understand it they do see  the news as well they do see that  high-dose opioids are dangerous they're  also motivated and actually frequently  we do see functional improvements in  pain  for our patients after they're they're  off of the high doses  that's a small warning though this paper  came out just a couple months ago  and showed that looking at a large  commercial insurance data set for  patients that did have tapers they saw  increases in the numbers of overdoses  and mental health crises associated with  those decreases in doses  now this paper was unable to look at  what else was offered such as the  psychosocial support that i mentioned  before but i just want to highlight that  these are unintentional consequences  that can be  the patients can have if you do embark  on a program like this needs to be done  very thoughtfully and you need to  embrace the patient and make sure they  have all the services they need  including for patients that that have  exacerbations of depression when they  stop their opioids or those that truly  do have opioid use disorder that need  addiction treatment in addition to their  pain treatment  and that brings us to the third  advanced concept which is substance use  disorder treatment  at our hospital i'm really proud of the  model that we we implemented which was  after a couple of other models that  happened throughout the country most  notably there was landmark work from  yale university which showed that if you  were able to start a patient who  presented to the emergency department  with withdrawal or seeking help for  their opioids on medication for opioid  use disorder such as suboxone or  buprenorphine  and get them into a clinic within the  next  24 to 72 hours next business day and  have them follow up you can over you can  you more than double their chances of  being  in active treatment at one month  the numbers went from around 30  to more than 70 percent  there's very little things in medicine  which are so effective so much effective  as this  and so we've we've adopted that same  model we have a bridge clinic it  basically will take patients on a  walk-in basis and an on-demand basis we  prefer that they do have an appointment  um you know we'll also do telehealth  especially in the age of coded but you  know what if a patient is still actively  using drugs  that's okay we just want to be able to  engage with them we want to be able to  share harm reduction resources with them  we want to be able to help them and when  they're ready when they're ready for  recovery we're there for them  and this model works because we know  that if you take a patient and  withdrawal and you tell them make an  appointment two weeks from thursday it's  not going to happen because the drugs  have hydra hijacked their brain and  they're not able to to do that so  patients do need a bridge  our bridge clinic has addiction  psychiatrists  medicine internal medicine physician  there's an id doctor on staff that  handles infectious disease complications  there are  case managers  care transition specialists that help  with long-term placement and it's just  been a very successful model that i  would encourage all of you to pursue  we've also another part of this was  getting prescribers to have their waiver  to prescribe buprenorphine as many of  you know that there previously was an  eight training course that was required  for  physicians that were prescribing  buprenorphine to their patients in a  24-hour  course for  advanced practice practitioners such as  nurse practitioners and physician  assistants  fortunately this past march those  training requirements went away  you still have to apply for the waiver  but at least you don't have to do long  training and that's to prescribe it to  30 patients at a time so this works for  the vast majority of our providers  whether they be in the emergency  department or in the hospital and that  was very helpful for us to be able to  get people waivered and also educate  them at the same time  and really the the innovative part of  this that i'm really proud of that i  didn't see coming but has has really  been transformational  is the idea that we can get people  started on medication for opioid use  disorder treatment  again buprenorphine most commonly  while they're in the hospital for  medical complications of injection drug  use  and we can send them home with a picc  line or a long-term indwelling  catheter  so the the prototypical patient would be  someone who had injected drugs they had  a bloodstream infection goes to their  heart and they get endocarditis they  typically need to need to be on about  six weeks of iv antibiotics  unfortunately the oral antibiotics just  don't work as well and iv is better  previously no one felt comfortable  sending those patients home with a picc  line in place because there was fear  that they would then inject through  their picc line  what we found is that if we can engage  patients while they're in the hospital  get them started on treatment have them  see a recovery coach  then have them follow up in the bridge  clinic at least twice a week afterwards  they can safely go home with a picc line  it's not for everybody we have to make  sure that they have stable housing  there's not active drug use in the house  that they've got transportation to come  back to the clinic that they're stable  on the medication before they leave the  hospital  but  our patients are very honest with us and  they tell us  we're not stupid i know that i might  have an addiction to drugs but i'm not  going to inject  drugs into this line that i know is  going directly into my heart  others have told us if i need to access  a vein to inject drugs i know how to do  that i don't need the pickling to do  that  so it's been a it's been a change it's  been uncomfortable for a lot of us but  we found through studies like this one  which you can you can look up or you can  reach out to me for a copy of that  patients actually do well with it  again not for everybody and it's not  perfect  we had 15 of patients relapse with our  initial cohort relapse is a part of  recovery and it's expected so that's not  failure that just means that we were we  know that a certain percentage will  relapse and we're there to help help  them when we can and when they're there  when they do relapse  the biggest sell for you for your  hospital your health system is days in  the hospital because previously we had  lots of difficulty  being able to transition these patients  to a rehab facility because many of them  wouldn't take patients with active  substance use disorder and so our  patients would end up going to the two  rehab facilities that would take  patients and we were told repetitively  that drug use there was rampant so it's  actually much worse for them to go to  these rehab facilities it's safer for  them to go home  another project we're working on is for  a pathway for patients that are admitted  with cardiac surgery you might have  heard this as well in the news of  patients that have cardiac surgery  because their valve became infected and  no longer works they have an open heart  surgery to fix their valve and then they  relapse reuse again and then  they need a repeat surgery  or when they're in the hospital we're so  focused on repairing their valve that we  don't treat the underlying issue we  don't treat the substance use disorder  which  is very is very unusual for me because  like if you have diabetes  and you might have a diabetic fluid  infection we don't just treat the  infection and not treat the underlying  diabetes  same thing with substance use disorders  so if we rolled out a pathway in which  we we have our nurses have a consistent  message we have communication with the  team so there's not splitting of  different providers or there's not  patients who come  who are told different things by  different providers we've actually  rolled this out this training out in our  simulation center and we've enacted  cases with our with our teams it's been  really successful  um and the last two things i want to  just mention are first about substitute  about national recovery month and the  final one is about stigma  national recovery month happens every  september and for us this is the time  when we reboot our initiatives we have  the majority of our of our information  booths our tables you can see there are  a picture on the top with two of our  recovery coaches  we have trainings like you can see in  the bottom we're doing a narcan training  session for for people in the hospital  that were walking by a highly trafficked  area  we have an exhibit a photojournalistic  exhibit about recovery  and then finally the one on the right  was a very moving exhibit we had this  year we planted purple flags one for  every life lost in our state in 2020 we  put it right on the main lawn in front  of the the original part of our hospital  things like this are a good moment to  spotlight recovery  and i would encourage you to embrace  this  there's a lot of materials available  online that you can you can use for your  own efforts  um and it again is a way to really  highlight this disease and that it is  treatable that recovery is possible  and the final part i want to mention is  just about stigma  unlike most any other disease  the stigma with this one is very  burdensome and we know that it's very  detrimental to care for patients who are  seeking help  and unlike other diseases where if you  say someone is diabetic it's not that  big of a deal  if you tell someone a medical  professional telling a patient that  they're an addict  it's a very it's  it's very hurtful to them it's um  it's putting a stigmatizing language on  that patient  we say that with other language as well  such as if you have a toxicology screen  that comes back positive and you call it  dirty  that implies that the patient is dirty  same thing if you say someone is clean  then what's the opposite of clean is  dirty so we really do things think that  words matter  there is a language that's used for  patients that are in recovery and they  might go to a narcotics anonymous  meeting and say that they are an addict  or they're an alcoholic and that that's  okay if they want to identify like that  with those words as part of their  recovery we should support that but as  medical professionals we need to be more  careful and so we rolled out a hospital  without stigma pledge you can see the  the terms there we're happy to share  this as well it's also available on our  website we just remind staff that this  this the words do matter with this  disease  and with that i'll conclude  um i'm happy to answer any questions you  might have by email that's my email  address there  please go to our websites we have two  one is b cord at brighamwomens.org which  has a lot of these  these guidelines  poppy.bwh.harvard.edu is our program in  opioid and pain innovation you can learn  about some of the work that we're doing  there and i also provided qr codes which  will get you directly to our opioid  prescribing guidelines and our opioid  tapering guidelines  i hope this was a helpful talk for you i  hope you enjoy the rest of the  conference and thank you so much for  your attention
 

